# Runtime status flags test fixtures (R7)

The AIC PIM workspace's long-running daemons emit
`runtime_status.json` (read by the configurator GUIs and the
`pim-offline-agent settings` subcommand) describing the daemon's
current effective configuration, last-sync time, in-flight HTTP
requests, and a `warnings[]` array. The post-2026-05-19 win32
configurator audit found that even when manifests had clean
defaults (R5) and the legacy migration ran (R3) and the GUID
normalizer collapsed magic sentinels (R6), an operator could
**still** end up running production with a fingerprint of a test
fixture if any link in the chain failed silently. This rule adds
the defense-in-depth check: at runtime, the daemon flags
fingerprints in its resolved configuration and surfaces them in
the configurator GUI so the operator sees them on the main
screen.

## The rule

> **Long-running daemons in this workspace emit a
> `warnings[]` row in `runtime_status.json` whenever any
> resolved setting matches one of the fingerprint patterns
> below. The row carries `kind` (the fingerprint pattern that
> matched), `field` (the manifest key name), and
> `value_fingerprint` (a SHA-256 hex digest, NEVER plaintext —
> see [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc)
> §5 masking contract). Configurator UIs (Win32 and Tauri) read
> the warnings count from `runtime_status.json` and surface it
> on the main screen as a non-dismissible badge until the
> warnings drop to zero.**

The check runs at daemon startup AND on every settings reload
(SIGHUP / `pim-offline-agent settings refresh`). It never blocks
the daemon's main loop; warnings are advisory data for the
operator.

## Fingerprint patterns

A resolved setting value (the string the daemon would actually
use, post-resolution) fires a warning if it matches any of:

| Pattern | Example match | `kind` field value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hostname suffix `.example` / `.example.com` / `.example.org` / `.example.net` | `server.example.com`, `db.example` | `placeholder_example_domain` |
| Hostname suffix `.test` / `.invalid` / `.localhost.test` | `tenant.test`, `agent.invalid` | `placeholder_test_domain` |
| Substring `do-not-ship` (case-insensitive) | `do-not-ship-token`, `DoNotShipKey` | `placeholder_do_not_ship` |
| Substring `test-secret` / `test_secret` / `testsecret` (case-insensitive) | `my-test-secret`, `TestSecret123` | `placeholder_test_secret` |
| Substring `changeme` / `change-me` / `change_me` (case-insensitive) | `password=changeme` | `placeholder_changeme` |
| Substring `XXXX` (4 or more consecutive Xs in a value that the manifest declares as a token / secret) | `bearer=XXXXabcdXXXX` | `placeholder_xxxx_token` |
| Exact `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000` | the zero GUID | `placeholder_zero_guid` |
| Exact `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001` | the zero-plus-one GUID | `placeholder_zero_plus_one_guid` |
| Exact `12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012` | the sequence GUID | `placeholder_sequence_guid` |
| Hex-only value (regex `^[0-9a-fA-F]+$`) shorter than 32 chars in a manifest field declared `kind = "secret"` | `bearer=deadbeef` (8 chars) | `weak_hex_secret` |
| Substring `localhost` in a `default_prod` resolution context (where the manifest's `prod_must_be_remote = true`) | `https://localhost:8443` resolved in a `default_prod` install | `placeholder_localhost_in_prod` |

Patterns can be extended in future minor releases. The list above
is the floor.

## Required emitted shape

`runtime_status.json` (the field exists; this rule pins the
`warnings[]` row schema):

```json
{
  "service": "pim-offline-agent",
  "version": "1.0.0-rc.1",
  "pid": 4812,
  "started_at": "2026-05-19T10:30:00Z",
  "last_sync_age_seconds": 142,
  "consecutive_failures": 0,
  "in_flight_requests": [],
  "warnings": [
    {
      "kind": "placeholder_example_domain",
      "field": "SERVER_PUBLIC_URL",
      "value_fingerprint": "0a4d55a8d778e5022fab701977c5d840bbc486d0f9e6b1ddc04b14ff5cef34d5",
      "first_observed_at": "2026-05-19T10:30:00Z"
    },
    {
      "kind": "placeholder_sequence_guid",
      "field": "DEFAULT_TENANT_GUID",
      "value_fingerprint": "5e884898da28047151d0e56f8dc6292773603d0d6aabbdd62a11ef721d1542d8",
      "first_observed_at": "2026-05-19T10:30:00Z"
    }
  ]
}
```

`value_fingerprint` is `SHA-256(value_bytes_utf8)` hex-encoded
lowercase. The plaintext value is NEVER written to
`runtime_status.json`. This matches the
[`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5
masking contract and lets two operators / SIEM agents correlate
"same value across hosts" without leaking the value.

## Required emit-site pattern

```rust
// pim-offline-client/src/runtime_status.rs (illustrative)
fn fingerprint_warnings(resolved: &ResolvedSettings) -> Vec<Warning> {
    let mut warnings = Vec::new();
    for (key, value) in resolved.iter_all() {
        if let Some(kind) = match_fingerprint(key, value) {
            warnings.push(Warning {
                kind,
                field: key.to_string(),
                value_fingerprint: sha256_hex(value.as_bytes()),
                first_observed_at: OffsetDateTime::now_utc(),
            });
        }
    }
    warnings
}
```

`match_fingerprint` consults the table above. The function is
pure and side-effect-free; it must NOT log the plaintext value
even at `trace` level (a future SIEM operator running the daemon
at `RUST_LOG=trace` must not see the value either).

## Required configurator-UI surface

Every configurator GUI in the workspace (Win32 admin × client +
server, Tauri admin × client + server) renders the warnings
count on the main screen:

- **Win32**: a status line on the main dialog `LTEXT` named
 `IDC_LBL_RUNTIME_WARNINGS_COUNT` reading `"⚠ 2 placeholder
 settings detected"` (or `""` when zero, control hidden).
- **Tauri**: a `<div role="status">` rendering the same text
 with `aria-live="polite"` so screen readers announce the
 count on first paint.

Both surfaces have a "Details" affordance that opens a
read-only dialog listing each warning by `field` and `kind`
(never the fingerprint hex — that's for SIEM, not the operator
UI). The dialog has a "Open settings" button that focuses the
first offending field in the settings tab.

The warnings count is non-dismissible: the operator clears it
only by fixing the underlying setting. This is intentional —
WACK and Section 508 testing both penalize "dismissible
warnings that hide critical state".

## Forbidden constructs

- A daemon that resolves settings without calling
 `fingerprint_warnings(&resolved)` before publishing
 `runtime_status.json`.
- A `warnings[]` row that carries the plaintext value in any
 field. Always fingerprint.
- A configurator GUI that suppresses the warnings count
 ("disable in test mode", "hide if user dismissed once") —
 the count is always rendered when non-zero.
- A `tracing::info!(value = %value, "starting with config")`
 line at startup that logs the resolved configuration map
 verbatim — secrets must be masked per the
 [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5
 masking helpers (`pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::*`)
 BEFORE logging.
- An extension to the fingerprint list that adds a pattern
 matching customer-supplied data shapes (e.g. `john@acme.com`
 as `placeholder_email`). The list is for **placeholder /
 test** fingerprints only; matching real user data would
 produce noise.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation** (today, conceptual):

```rust
// runtime_status.rs
fn emit(resolved: &ResolvedSettings) {
    let snapshot = RuntimeStatusSnapshot {
        service: "pim-offline-agent".into(),
        version: env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION").into(),
        // ... other fields ...
        warnings: vec![], // never populated
    };
    write_json("runtime_status.json", &snapshot);
}
```

The operator's `SERVER_PUBLIC_URL` resolved to
`https://server.example.com:8443` because the manifest default
leaked into prod (R5). The daemon happily starts. The
configurator shows zero warnings. The first agent sync fails
with `dns lookup`, and the operator spends 40 minutes diagnosing
a DNS / firewall issue when the actual cause is a leaked
placeholder.

**Fix**:

```rust
fn emit(resolved: &ResolvedSettings) {
    let warnings = fingerprint_warnings(resolved);
    if !warnings.is_empty() {
        tracing::warn!(
            count = warnings.len(),
            fields = ?warnings.iter().map(|w| &w.field).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            "runtime placeholder warnings present; operator should review"
        );
    }
    let snapshot = RuntimeStatusSnapshot {
        service: "pim-offline-agent".into(),
        version: env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION").into(),
        // ... other fields ...
        warnings,
    };
    write_json("runtime_status.json", &snapshot);
}
```

The configurator main screen now shows
`⚠ 1 placeholder setting detected` immediately after the
daemon starts. The operator opens "Details", sees
`SERVER_PUBLIC_URL: placeholder_example_domain`, fixes the
value, restarts the service, and the badge clears.

